 ],
 "literal": "To the street to reach, gently having come, having come / rarely, opening his mouth, sweet words having spoken, / every day, with his complexion faded, he who stays, / his sorrowful look, do you not consider, O friend? / Of the sweet, strong toddy, Akutai's father, / with white-tipped small staffs, the singer's women, / a gentle female elephant as a gift, resembling, / intending something else, his standing outside for a long time!",
 "translation": "O friend, do you not consider his sorrowful look? Every day he comes gently to our street, and with great difficulty, speaks a few sweet words, his complexion fading as he stays. Like the women of the bards with white-tipped small staffs who sing for Akutai's father—the lord of sweet, strong toddy—and stand waiting, expecting a gentle female elephant as a gift, he stands outside our house for a long time, clearly intending something else.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! நம் தெருவை அடைந்து, மெதுவாக மீண்டும் மீண்டும் வந்து, அரிதாகத் தன் வாயைத் திறந்து, இனிய சொற்களைக் கூறி, நாள்தோறும் தன் நிறம் மாறி வாடி நிற்கும் அவனுடைய துன்பம் நிறைந்த பார்வையை நீ நினைக்க மாட்டாயா? இனிய, கடுமையான கள்ளையுடைய அகுதையின் தந்தையிடம், வெண்மையான நுனியையுடைய சிறிய கோலைக் கொண்ட பாடினிப் பெண்கள், இளமையான பெண் யானையைப் பரிசாகப் பெற விரும்பி நிற்பது போல, அவன் வேறு ஏதோ ஒன்றை (உன் திருமணத்தை) மனதில் கொண்டு நம் வீட்டின் நீண்ட புறத்தே வந்து காத்து நிற்கும் நிலையைக் காண்பாயாக.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the confidante to the heroine in a Kuriñci context, urging her to accept the hero's love and agree to marriage. The hero has been visiting the heroine's street secretly, speaking sweet words with difficulty, and waiting outside for long periods. His complexion is fading due to the distress of unfulfilled love and the difficulty of secret trysts. The confidante compares his long, patient waiting outside to the bardic women who carry white-tipped staffs and sing praises to the chieftain Akutai's father, waiting patiently to receive a grand gift like a female elephant. Just as the bards stand waiting with a specific reward in mind, the hero stands outside intending 'something else'—which is the hand of the heroine in marriage.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
